## Purpose

Build a deterministic, low-reasoning qualification harness that drives synthetic Tool Shed work
through real lifecycle routes and proves that local authority, reporter and hosted projections,
and operator-visible status agree at every material checkpoint. The first regression target is the
phantom development-dashboard projects that demonstrated healthy components can still present
materially false fleet state.

## Desired Outcome

- A fixed nine-scenario corpus exercises Idea, readiness, PRM, governed work, evidence, recursive
  closure, reconciliation, checkpoint/rebuild, reporting, hosted storage, and critical browser UI.
- An independent oracle reconstructs lineage, blockers, evidence validity, and effective closure
  from canonical records without importing the product rollup evaluator.
- Every checkpoint proves expected presence and zero unexpected rows across local, transport,
  hosted, and presentation layers.
- Failures retain a bounded redacted replay recipe and may be minimized deterministically without
  mutating enrolled fixtures or changing the failure signature.
- The Linux, Windows, and hosted-development lanes qualify one exact candidate before any separate
  production-promotion decision.

## System Boundary

| Surface | Authority or role | Qualification responsibility |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Local Tool Shed database | Canonical document, cycle, requirement, lineage, evidence, closure, and reconciliation facts | Drive guarded mutations and independently inspect exact revisions and residue |
| Reporter and hosted database | Ordered transport records and advisory hosted projections | Prove identity, sequence, idempotency, convergence, freshness, and exact cardinality |
| Dashboard and browser | Operator presentation only | Prove inventory, detail closure/blockers, attention/freshness, filters, counts, and links |
| Harness records | Versioned scenario intent and generated replay evidence | Bind candidate, fixture, platform, seed, actions, checkpoints, verdict, and retained evidence |
| Fault environment | Verified disposable copies and a future development-only hosted namespace | Contain corruption, reject production and operational identities, and preserve tombstones |

